ed as failures, so the cron keeps re-processing them every run until `scheduledDate` passes. That is the exact behaviour the PR sets out to remove. A missing phone number is a common case of the 'invalid phone number' scenario in the description."}, {"file": "packages/features/ee/workflows/api/scheduleSMSReminders.ts", "line": 184, "severity": "low", "category": "bug", "title": "Read-modify-write retryCount update is not atomic", "body": "`retryCount: reminder.retryCount + 1` uses the value read at the start of the run. If two cron invocations overlap, both read the same count and both write count+1, so a failure is undercounted and the cutoff is delayed. Prisma's atomic `retryCount: { increment: 1 }` avoids this and is the idiomatic form (the same applies to the catch block at line 195)."}, {"file": "packages/features/ee/workflows/api/scheduleSMSReminders.ts", "line": 39, "severity": "low", "category": "perf", "title": "Unindexed retryCount in an OR condition on a large table", "body": "The delete's WHERE is now `(method AND scheduledDate) OR retryCount > 1`. The existing indexes (`[method, scheduled, scheduledDate]`, etc.) cannot serve the `retryCount` branch, so Postgres will likely fall back to a sequential scan of WorkflowReminder on every cron run. Scoping the second branch to `method = SMS` and `scheduled = false` would let the existing index apply."}, {"file": "packages/features/ee/workflows/api/scheduleSMSReminders.ts", "line": 178, "severity": "low", "category": "test_gap", "title": "No tests for the new retry/delete behaviour", "body": "The PR adds retry counting and a new deletion rule: a failed `scheduleSMS` increments retryCount, and rows with retryCount > 1 are deleted on the next run. None of this is covered by a test, including the off-by-one threshold (`gt: 1` means deletion after the second failure). A regression in the counting or the OR delete filter would go unnoticed."}]}
